andlord might waste a Joker or Bomb, making him weak later. If the Landlord doesn't have a good response, my teammate, who has a big hand, takes the lead and runs their hand. That's what you want, in this position! This scenario is the best because I can just be safe here with my pairs and small cards!\n\nSo, the optimal move is clear: Pass. The Teammate may now win and has the lead. I don't interfere, I support. It is always a team game. It's the only logical move, the only move that makes strategic sense. Let's send that JSON and move on!\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current trick history is Me (10) -> Landlord (J) -> Teammate (2). My teammate has played a very high card (2) to take control of the trick. Beating this card with my Black Joker would be a significant mistake for two reasons: 1) It wastes my strongest card to beat my own partner, and 2) It relieves the pressure on the Landlord (who acts next). By passing, I force the Landlord to either spend a top resource (Red Joker or Bomb) to beat the 2 or concede the lead to my teammate. My hand (pairs of 6, J, Q, and singles A, 2, Black Joker) is strong enough to support later, but right now, stepping aside is the optimal cooperative play.\n\nReason: My teammate has played a 2, which is the highest non-Joker card, specifically to beat the Landlord\'s Jack. Since the Landlord acts immediately after me, I must pass to force the Landlord to either burn a high-value card (Red Joker) to win or allow my teammate to take the lead. Playing the Black Joker here would be counterproductive as it neutralizes my teammate\'s strong play and wastes my best card.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
